│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_start_greedy_best_of_n                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current code runs a single greedy pass with full sampling (sample_rate=1.0), so the   │
│                          │ result depends heavily on one random starting transaction. Since greedy is cheap per      │
│                          │ restart, running many randomized restarts (with partial sampling to add diversity) and    │
│                          │ keeping the best schedule found dramatically reduces makespan. Additionally, after        │
│                          │ picking each transaction we keep the best-so-far across all restarts. This "best-of-N     │
│                          │ randomized greedy" is a standard and very effective metaheuristic for sequencing problems │
│                          │ like this, and directly optimizes the actual makespan via `get_opt_seq_cost` rather than  │
│                          │ heuristics like transaction length.                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key changes:                                                                              │
│                          │ 1. Reduce `num_samples` per step slightly (10 -> 6) to allow more restarts within the     │
│                          │ same budget.                                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Use a sample_rate < 1 (0.85) so occasionally a random transaction is appended, adding  │
│                          │ diversity between restarts.                                                               │
│                          │ 3. Run N restarts (e.g., 40) and return the schedule with the lowest overall makespan.    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
