│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ best_of_n_greedy_random_restarts                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current implementation runs a single greedy pass with a fixed sample of 10 candidates │
│                          │ per step and no restarts, so a single bad early pick can lock in a poor schedule. Since   │
│                          │ get_greedy_cost_sampled is randomized, we can run many independent greedy restarts with   │
│                          │ slightly varied sampling rates and keep the best schedule found. This is essentially a    │
│                          │ "GRASP"-style (Greedy Randomized Adaptive Search Procedure) approach: each restart        │
│                          │ explores a different region of the ordering space, and taking the best-of-N dramatically  │
│                          │ lowers the expected makespan without changing per-run cost structure. We also lower the   │
│                          │ per-step sample count to 5 so we can afford more restarts within a similar compute        │
│                          │ budget, since the best-of-many effect dominates the per-step sample quality. To stay      │
│                          │ within reasonable time, we cap the number of restarts (e.g., 150) and track the best      │
│                          │ makespan/schedule across restarts, returning the overall best. This directly reduces the  │
│                          │ makespan metric while keeping schedules valid (each greedy pass already asserts a         │
│                          │ complete permutation).                                                                    │
