│ patch_name               │ beam_grasp_perturb_search                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of pure greedy restarts followed by a single 2-opt pass, I implement a            │
│                          │ GRASP-style framework: (1) beam search construction that keeps multiple partial schedules │
│                          │ alive at each step, allowing recovery from greedy mistakes; (2) repeated                  │
│                          │ randomized-greedy restarts with a restricted candidate list; (3) local search on every    │
│                          │ promising candidate using both 2-opt segment reversal and insertion moves (moving a txn   │
│                          │ to a different position, which 2-opt cannot do); (4) perturbation kicks (random           │
│                          │ double-bridge moves) from the incumbent to escape local optima, accepting only            │
│                          │ improvements. The key insight is that 2-opt reversal alone is weak for this               │
│                          │ problem—relocating a single conflicted transaction often reduces makespan far more—so     │
│                          │ combining insertion moves with kick-based restarts from good solutions beats searching    │
│                          │ from scratch each time.                                                                   │
