│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ in_place_two_opt_local_search                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current local search only performs adjacent swaps, which is a very weak neighborhood  │
│                          │ — it can barely reorder the greedy construction. A 2-opt (segment reversal) neighborhood  │
│                          │ explores a much larger portion of the schedule space. The recommendation asks us to avoid │
│                          │ rebuilding full candidate lists every iteration (as the sliced-copy approach does) and    │
│                          │ instead apply/undo reversals in place on a single mutable sequence, which cuts constant   │
│                          │ overhead and allows more improvement rounds within the same time budget.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Step-by-step reasoning:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Replace `local_search` with a 2-opt routine operating on a single list: reverse `seq`  │
│                          │ in place, evaluate with `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, and if the move does not improve,    │
│                          │ reverse it back to undo the change — no list copies at all.                               │
│                          │ 2. First-improvement strategy: accept the first improving reversal, restart the scan from │
│                          │ that position (don't need a full restart), and keep iterating until no improving move     │
│                          │ exists.                                                                                   │
│                          │ 3. Wrap the 2-opt around the multi-restart greedy: run 30 greedy restarts, 2-opt improve  │
│                          │ each candidate briefly, keep the global best, then run a deeper 2-opt pass on the global  │
│                          │ best. Since 2-opt on in-place reversals is cheap, we can afford to improve every restart  │
│                          │ rather than only the final winner, giving better basins for the final deep polish.        │
│                          │ 4. Because the schedule is a permutation of transaction indices, any reversal is still a  │
│                          │ valid permutation, so validity is preserved and makespan evaluation via                   │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost` is unchanged.                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should reduce makespan below the current 343 because adjacent swaps are a strict     │
│                          │ subset of 2-opt moves; the larger neighborhood escapes local optima the swap-only search  │
│                          │ gets stuck in, and the in-place apply/undo lets us run many more evaluations within the   │
│                          │ time budget.                                                                              │
