                        Patch Metadata - Gen 20/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ iterated_local_search_from_best                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm runs independent greedy restarts, each polished by local search.    │
│                          │ Independent restarts waste time: each greedy construction lands in a different region and │
│                          │ the local search must re-polish from scratch. A better strategy is Iterated Local Search  │
│                          │ (ILS): keep the current best solution as the anchor, generate each new candidate by       │
│                          │ perturbing it (remove a random contiguous segment of transactions and greedily reinsert   │
│                          │ them at the best positions), then apply the local search to the perturbed candidate. This │
│                          │ concentrates the search effort around the best-so-far basin while still providing enough  │
│                          │ diversification via the random segment removal. Over a fixed time budget, ILS reliably    │
│                          │ outperforms independent restarts for permutation problems like this one.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Reasoning step by step:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Greedy construction from scratch gives quality but with high variance; local search    │
│                          │ alone gets stuck in local optima.                                                         │
│                          │ 2. Perturbing the best solution (ruin a random window of 3–8 transactions, recreate       │
│                          │ greedily) creates a candidate close to the incumbent, so the local search only needs a    │
│                          │ few improving moves rather than dozens.                                                   │
│                          │ 3. Accepting only improvements to the incumbent (strict ILS) means search effort          │
│                          │ accumulates monotonically around the best region found.                                   │
│                          │ 4. Keeping the local search with a per-candidate time slice ensures we don't spend the    │
│                          │ whole budget on one candidate.                                                            │
