│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ ruin_recreate_perturbation                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The current program runs a fixed 30 greedy restarts and then one local search descent, so │
│                          │ it can only escape local optima via Or-opt moves of length ≤3. I propose two changes: (1) │
│                          │ wrap the whole pipeline in a time budget (~25s) so restarts and refinement adapt to       │
│                          │ actual runtime rather than a fixed count; (2) add a ruin-and-recreate perturbation loop — │
│                          │ remove 3–8 random transactions, greedily reinsert each at its best position, and run the  │
│                          │ local search on the perturbed solution, accepting it only if it improves the best. This   │
│                          │ is a well-known Large Neighborhood Search pattern: ruining breaks the structural bias of  │
│                          │ the greedy construction while greedy reinsertion keeps solutions high-quality, letting    │
│                          │ the descent escape local minima that swap/Or-opt cannot. Empirically this gains           │
│                          │ significant makespan over plain local moves at identical construction settings.           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0091                                                                                   │
