├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ incremental_delta_sa                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The prior programs all bottleneck on calling the expensive full-schedule evaluator        │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost` for every candidate move, limiting SA to a few thousand iterations.    │
│                          │ Inspired by the recommendation about incremental cost evaluation, this version exploits   │
│                          │ the workload's `get_incremental_seq_cost(txn, key_map, cost)` primitive (seen referenced  │
│                          │ in the earliest context program) to build a fast incremental evaluator: a sequence's cost │
│                          │ is computed by threading a (key_map, cost) state through one incremental call per         │
│                          │ transaction. At startup we verify this incremental cost exactly matches                   │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost` on random permutations; if it does, all greedy construction and SA     │
│                          │ move evaluation use the fast path, giving roughly an order-of-magnitude more search       │
│                          │ iterations. The greedy constructor now evaluates **every** remaining candidate per step   │
│                          │ (previously only 10 random samples) since each evaluation is now cheap, yielding much     │
│                          │ better starting solutions. The SA then runs many more iterations with                     │
│                          │ insertion/swap/segment moves, plus stagnation kicks reheating from the best solution. If  │
│                          │ the incremental API is unavailable or inconsistent, we fall back to the original          │
│                          │ evaluator so correctness is preserved.                                                    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
