╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ tune_sa_params_and_reheat_from_greedy_pool                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed relative to the best prior generation (285 vs 251           │
│                          │ makespan). Diagnosis: (1) the temperature schedule changed to T0 = 2% of cost with alpha  │
│                          │ = 0.99995, which cools far too slowly — the SA spends most of its budget at high          │
│                          │ temperature doing near-random walks instead of exploiting the good greedy solution; the   │
│                          │ best-performing generation used T0 = 1% and a faster cooldown, letting the search settle  │
│                          │ into deep minima. (2) The stagnation kick always restarts from `best_seq`, so after the   │
│                          │ search gets stuck it never revisits alternative greedy basins — keeping a small pool of   │
│                          │ distinct good greedy solutions and occasionally kicking from a random pool member         │
│                          │ restores basin diversity. (3) The greedy phase gets 35% of the budget but greedy          │
│                          │ solutions are quickly surpassed by SA; trimming it to 25% gives SA more time at           │
│                          │ productive (mid-to-low) temperatures.                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Reasoning step-by-step: cost values here are ~100-scale makespans, so a delta of 1-5 is   │
│                          │ typical between neighbors; with T ≈ 2 initially (2% of ~100), worsening moves of delta 3  │
│                          │ are accepted with probability e^-1.5 ≈ 0.22 — fine — but with alpha = 0.99995 and only    │
│                          │ tens of thousands of iterations within 40s, T only drops to ~30-50% of T0, never reaching │
│                          │ the low-temperature exploitation regime where SA behaves like a proper local descent.     │
│                          │ Lowering T0 to 1% and cooling with alpha = 0.9995 brings T down to ~5% of T0 within ~6000 │
│                          │ iterations, so the bulk of the budget is spent refining rather than randomizing. Kicks    │
│                          │ from the greedy pool (rather than always the incumbent best) implement a simple           │
│                          │ population-based iterated local search that escapes deep local optima without losing the  │
│                          │ best solution found (still tracked separately). These changes directly target the         │
│                          │ makespan quality of the final schedule.                                                   │
