│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ perturb_reinsert_restarts                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current greedy phase always builds sequences from scratch with a random starting      │
│                          │ transaction, which wastes trials exploring basins far from the incumbent. Following the   │
│                          │ recommendation, I add a "shuffled best" restart mechanism: every 4th trial, instead of a  │
│                          │ fresh greedy construction, take the current best sequence, remove 4–6 random              │
│                          │ transactions, and greedily reinsert each at its cheapest remaining position using         │
│                          │ full-cost evaluation (`get_opt_seq_cost`). This is cost-guided diversity starting near a  │
│                          │ proven-good solution — a form of iterated local search during construction. It's cheaper  │
│                          │ per trial than a full greedy pass (only ~5 reinsertions of ~5 position evaluations each   │
│                          │ vs. building the whole sequence), so more restarts fit in the 60% construction slice. The │
│                          │ incumbent-based perturbation explores neighborhoods the adjacent-swap and insertion SA    │
│                          │ phases handle poorly during construction, likely yielding better starting points for the  │
│                          │ SA refiner and thus lower makespan.                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Reasoning step by step:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Greedy from random starts is high-variance; most trials land far from optimal.         │
│                          │ 2. Perturbing the incumbent (removing a few txns, greedy reinsertion) generates           │
│                          │ candidates in the incumbent's basin — classic iterated local search, which converges much │
│                          │ faster on permutation-style scheduling problems.                                          │
│                          │ 3. Mixed with the existing full-greedy/randomized trials (trial % 4 == 0 instead of       │
│                          │ building from scratch), we keep global exploration while adding local intensification     │
│                          │ during construction.                                                                      │
│                          │ 4. The SA phase then refines an already-better incumbent.                                 │
