                        Patch Metadata - Gen 40/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ ruin_recreate_plus_sa                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current program spends most of its 20s budget on expensive full greedy constructions  │
│                          │ (each O(n²) full-sequence evaluations) and only reaches the ruin-and-recreate stage once  │
│                          │ or twice per budget. Since ruin-and-recreate with perturbation was the pattern that       │
│                          │ historically gained the most makespan (Gen 20: +23 units), I restructure the budget:      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Fast construction phase (~2s):** Run a few greedy constructions with perturbation,   │
│                          │ but immediately cap each with local search so we get a good incumbent quickly.            │
│                          │ 2. **Ruin-and-recreate with SA acceptance (~13s):** Instead of accepting only             │
│                          │ improvements (which stalls), use simulated-annealing acceptance: accept slightly worse    │
│                          │ reconstructions with probability exp(-Δ/T), with temperature geometrically decreasing and │
│                          │ periodic reheating. This escapes local minima while intensifying near the end. Removing   │
│                          │ 3–8 transactions and greedily reinserting each at its cheapest position is a              │
│                          │ large-neighborhood move that explores far more than pairwise swaps per evaluation.        │
│                          │ 3. **Final intensification (~5s):** Low-temperature SA on relocation/swap moves,          │
│                          │ first-improvement descent at near-zero temperature.                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Time checks are done inside the insertion loops so the deadline is respected. Best        │
│                          │ incumbent is always tracked and returned. This fills the gap between the greedy tier      │
│                          │ (~356) and the SA tier (~244) by giving the LNS perturbation the bulk of the budget with  │
│                          │ annealing acceptance rather than improvement-only acceptance.                             │
